THE NODE.JS THROUGHPUT PROBLEM:
Node.js executes JavaScript on a single V8 thread. There is one thread
and it does everything: runs your application code, handles callbacks,
processes Promise resolutions, and executes gRPC client code. Node.js
is not multi-threaded from JavaScript's perspective.

Per-request cost breakdown for a Spanner read in Node.js today:

  JavaScript CPU work (V8 thread, blocking):
    Serialize params into gRPC request object:  ~20–40µs
    grpc-js internal JavaScript overhead:       ~15–25µs
    Deserialize Protobuf response to JS objects:~40–80µs
    Total V8-blocking CPU time per request:     ~75–145µs

  Network + Spanner processing:                 ~3–6ms
    (handled by libuv native thread pool
     underneath Node — V8 thread is theoretically
     free here but only if JS is not blocking it)

At approximately 3000–5000 QPS, the single V8 thread spends 100% of
its time on JavaScript-layer CPU overhead and cannot keep up with
incoming work. New requests queue inside the event loop. Latency
climbs. Throughput plateaus. No amount of configuration or tuning
can raise this ceiling because the bottleneck is the single V8 thread.

THE FIX:
Move the per-request JavaScript CPU work — Protobuf encode and decode,
gRPC dispatch coordination — into a Rust native extension. The Rust
extension is compiled to a .node binary file that Node.js loads and
calls like a normal JavaScript module. Inside the extension, a Tokio
async runtime handles the gRPC connection and network I/O on its own
thread pool, completely separate from Node's V8 thread and libuv pool.

The V8 thread hands a request to the Rust extension, receives a Promise
back immediately (non-blocking), and is free to handle other work. When
Rust/Tokio completes the network round-trip, it calls back into V8 via
a thread-safe callback to resolve the Promise. From JavaScript this looks
like a completely normal async function call.

V8 thread CPU time per request drops from ~75–145µs to ~10–15µs.
At high concurrency this means the V8 thread is never saturated,
latency stays flat, and throughput scales significantly beyond the
JavaScript-layer ceiling.

THE BINDING LAYER — napi-rs:
napi-rs is a Rust crate that provides safe, ergonomic bindings between
Rust code and the Node.js N-API (Node API). N-API is a stable C API
that Node.js exposes for native extensions — stable means a .node file
compiled for Node 18 works on Node 20 and 22 without recompilation.
napi-rs wraps the N-API in safe Rust code and handles:
  - JavaScript ↔ Rust type conversion (JS string → Rust String, etc)
  - Promise creation and resolution from Rust code
  - ThreadsafeFunction: calling back into V8 from a Tokio thread
  - AsyncTask: running work on a background thread, resolving a Promise

THE TOKIO RUNTIME:
Tokio is Rust's async runtime. It manages a pool of OS threads that
execute async Rust tasks. When the Rust extension receives a request,
it spawns an async task onto Tokio. Tokio sends the gRPC request over
the network, waits for the response, decodes the Protobuf, and calls
back into V8 when done. Tokio's threads are entirely separate from
Node's V8 thread and libuv thread pool. They coexist without conflict.

THE gRPC LIBRARY — tonic:
tonic is a Rust gRPC client library built on Tokio. It handles HTTP/2
connection management, TLS, and Protobuf-encoded request/response over
the wire. One tonic Channel is a persistent HTTP/2 connection that
multiplexes hundreds of concurrent request streams. There is no need
for multiple channels — one channel is sufficient for any throughput
level because HTTP/2 multiplexing handles concurrent streams natively.

THE PROTOBUF LIBRARY — prost:
prost is a Rust Protobuf library. It compiles .proto files into Rust
structs at build time via a build script (build.rs). These structs
represent Spanner API request and response messages. Encoding a request
into Protobuf bytes and decoding a response from Protobuf bytes happens
entirely in Rust, with zero JavaScript involvement.

WHAT THE POC MUST PROVE:
  1. V8 thread CPU time per request decreases measurably
  2. Throughput scales beyond the JavaScript-layer ceiling
  3. p50 and p99 latency improve at high concurrency levels
  4. The V8 event loop stays responsive under load (low event loop lag)
  5. QPS scales near-linearly with concurrency up to network limits

WHAT THE POC DOES NOT NEED:
  - Session pool management in Rust (reuse existing JS session pool)
  - Transaction support (read-only benchmark queries only)
  - Authentication in Rust (reuse existing google-auth-library tokens)
  - Production error handling, retry logic, or circuit breaking
  - Windows builds (Linux x86_64 and macOS arm64 only for POC)
  - Any change to the public @google-cloud/spanner JavaScript API
  - The existing @google-cloud/spanner library is not modified at all

