════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
CRITICAL ARCHITECTURAL CONSTRAINT — DO NOT VIOLATE
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

THE V8 EVENT LOOP THREAD MUST NEVER BE BLOCKED.

This is the single most important constraint in this entire POC.
If you block the V8 thread you defeat the entire purpose.

Node.js is built on the assumption that JavaScript never blocks.
If JavaScript blocks (e.g. by calling a synchronous function that
waits for I/O), the entire event loop stalls. All pending Promises,
all incoming requests, all timers — everything stops until the
blocking call returns. This is catastrophic for a server application.

WRONG — this blocks the V8 thread, do not use:
  // This pattern is WRONG for Node.js
  #[napi]
  fn execute_sql(session: String, sql: String, token: String)
      -> Vec<Vec<String>> {
      // block_on() on V8 thread = entire event loop stalls
      RUNTIME.block_on(async {
          client.execute_sql(request).await
      })
  }

CORRECT — AsyncTask runs work on background thread, returns Promise:
  #[napi]
  fn execute_sql_native(
      session_name: String,
      sql: String,
      token: String,
  ) -> AsyncTask<SpannerTask> {
      // Returns immediately — napi-rs wraps this in a Promise
      // The actual work happens in SpannerTask::compute()
      // which runs on a background thread, not V8 thread
      AsyncTask::new(SpannerTask { session: session_name, sql, token })
  }

The napi-rs AsyncTask pattern works as follows:
  - JavaScript calls executeSqlNative(session, sql, token)
  - napi-rs creates a Promise and returns it to JavaScript immediately
  - napi-rs schedules SpannerTask on a background thread (libuv pool)
  - SpannerTask::compute() runs on the background thread:
      it calls RUNTIME.block_on() which is SAFE here because
      this is a background thread, not the V8 thread
  - When compute() returns, napi-rs calls SpannerTask::resolve()
      back on the V8 thread to convert Rust types to JS objects
  - The Promise resolves with the result
  - JavaScript awaits the Promise — non-blocking in JS

