KNOWN ISSUES — HANDLE THESE PROACTIVELY
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1. Proto transitive dependency imports:
   Spanner protos import these transitive dependencies:
     google/api/annotations.proto
     google/api/client.proto
     google/api/field_behavior.proto
     google/api/resource.proto
     google/protobuf/struct.proto     (in prost-types crate)
     google/protobuf/timestamp.proto  (in prost-types crate)
     google/rpc/status.proto
   
   The tonic-build include path must be set to /tmp/googleapis
   so all google/api/ and google/rpc/ imports resolve correctly.
   The google/protobuf/ well-known types are bundled inside
   the prost-types crate — tonic-build finds them automatically
   via the OUT_DIR environment variable set by cargo.
   
   If compilation fails with "import not found", check that
   /tmp/googleapis was cloned successfully and contains the
   google/api/ directory.

2. napi-rs generated index.js file:
   After running napi build, napi-rs generates an index.js file
   in the package root. This file detects the platform at runtime
   and requires the correct platform-specific .node file.
   poc_bridge.js must require this index.js, not the .node file
   directly, because the .node filename contains the platform
   (e.g. spanner-native.linux-x64-gnu.node).
   
   Correct: const spannerNative = require('../index.js')
   Wrong:   const spannerNative = require('../spanner-native.node')

3. Session pool internal API variations:
   The @google-cloud/spanner session pool exposes database.pool_
   as its internal pool object. The acquire method may be named
   differently across library versions:
     Try: await this.database.pool_.acquire()
     Fallback: await this.database.pool_.getSession()
   
   Use try/catch to attempt both and throw a descriptive error
   if neither works, explaining this is a POC internal API access
   and the user may need to check their library version.
   
   CRITICAL: always release the session back to the pool.
   Use try/finally to guarantee release even if an error occurs.
   Leaking sessions causes the pool to exhaust under sustained load
   and the benchmark will fail with session-related errors.

4. SpannerTask must implement Send:
   napi-rs requires AsyncTask implementations to be Send
   (safe to move between threads). The SpannerTask struct
   contains only String fields which are Send automatically.
   Do not add any Rc<T>, RefCell<T>, or raw pointer fields
   as these are not Send and will cause a compile error.

5. Token refresh concurrency:
   google-auth-library's getAccessToken() is safe to call
   concurrently — it internally handles caching and deduplicates
   concurrent refresh requests. No lock or mutex is needed in
   _getFreshToken(). Simply call it on every request.

6. TLS on corporate network or VM:
   Some Linux VMs on corporate networks require the corporate
   proxy CA certificate to be trusted. The code tries loading
   system CA bundles from common paths before falling back to
   bundled Mozilla roots. If TLS still fails, the user may need
   to add their corporate CA certificate to the system bundle.

7. process.exit() after benchmark:
   The @google-cloud/spanner library keeps background timers
   running (session keepalive, metrics). Without process.exit(0)
   the Node process will hang after the benchmark completes.
   Always call process.exit(0) at the end of main().

8. Benchmark event loop lag and the Rust path:
   At high concurrency the Rust path should show near-zero
   event loop lag because the V8 thread is freed during all
   network I/O. The JS path will show increasing lag as the
   V8 thread becomes saturated. If both paths show high lag,
   the AsyncTask implementation is incorrectly blocking V8.
   This is the most important correctness signal in the benchmark.

